Am I the only one who is worried about performance in all this discussion?
You know, just because NetHack is a somewhat older text-interface program
doesn't mean it isn't _huge_ and somewhat bloaty already. The memory
requirements are already somewhat significant. When playing a longer game,
I sometimes get a noticable delay when changing levels. Saving and loading
savefiles can take seconds. A textbased savegame format (being the logical
consequence of textbased bones) would multiply that. A full Java
application with an XML parser -- I don't even want to think about it.

NetHack is already being ported to a multitude of platforms. C _is_ more or
less portable. Save and bones compatibility are a (minor) PITA, granted,
but it could even be possible (although somewhat ugly) to implement a 
portable binary format in C. Many people play NetHack on systems without
the abundance of resources typical for modern PCs. My home machine isn't
all that fancy, and I used to play on a decades-old laptop and a sharp
zaurus PDA. If the next version at NH was Java only, I'd switch to another
roguelike on the spot.

